  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe strategy leverages hierarchical optimization with gradient descent on a sparse representation of h. By starting with a coarse grid and progressively refining it, we balance exploration of the solution space with efficient local optimization. This approach aims to avoid local minima while maintaining feasible solutions through a robust repair mechanism.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Variables: h(x) \u2208 [0, 1], sum(h) = n_points / 2\n- Objective: Minimize C\u2085 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1 - h(x+k)) dx\n- Constraints: \u222bh(x) dx = 1, 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1\n- Implementation as a minimax problem using hierarchical grid refinement.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initialize h: Start with initial_h_values if available, or a uniform distribution.\nb. Coarsen h to a lower resolution (e.g., from 100 to 10 intervals).\nc. Optimize the coarse h using gradient-based methods (e.g., L-BFGS).\nd. Refine h to the next level, using the optimized coarse solution.\ne. Repeat until reaching 100 intervals.\nf. Perform intensive gradient descent on the full 100-interval h.\ng. Use a repair function to maintain constraints after each optimization step.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Initial resolution: 10 intervals, coarsest level.\n- Hierarchical steps: 10 \u2192 20 \u2192 40 \u2192 100 intervals.\n- Optimization method: L-BFGS with gradient computed at current maximum lag.\n- Learning rate: Adaptive, based on optimization performance.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Hierarchical optimization reduces the dimensionality at each step, saving computational resources.\n- The process is designed to terminate gracefully within the 600s budget, returning the best found solution.\n- Repair mechanisms ensure feasibility at every step, preventing invalid candidates.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- After optimization, validate h against constraints: sum(h) == 1, 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1.\n- Compute C\u2085 using the provided evaluator to ensure consistency with the original problem statement.\n- Return the optimized h, the computed C\u2085 value, and the number of points (100).",
